{"key": "927e57d5866565439fee294324c02ad8561953e8", "pdf": "Electric_Motors/CORRUPT_BATTSBEVHEV.pdf", "pdf_name": "CORRUPT_BATTSBEVHEV.pdf", "mod": "MIL1", "slug": "corrupt-battsbevhev", "img_dir": "corrupt-battsbevhev-927e57d5", "title": "BATTERIES AND INTRO TO BEV/HEV Batteries", "pages": 28, "slides": [{"n": 1, "title": "BATTERIES AND INTRO TO BEV/HEV Batteries", "body": "Device used for storing electrochemical energy\nThe elementary element is the\ncell\n.\nThe principle consists in exploiting oxidation reaction at the negative pole\n«anode»\n,\ngenerating\nan excess of electrons and a reduction\nreaction at the positive pole\n«cathode»\nwhere the\nelectrons are «absorbed». The electron\nflow\n(current)\nthat can supply a load.\n��\n−\n��\n−\n��\n−\n��\n−\n��\n−\n��\n−\ne\n−\n+ Li\n������\n��\n+ Li\n+\n+ C6\n←\n������\n��\n+\n+ e\n−\n→ LiCoO2\n−\n��\n−\n��\n−\n�� −", "img": "p-001.webp"}, {"n": 2, "title": "Batteries Main characteristics", "body": "��\nM\nE\n����������2\n������2\n��\n������6\n����\n+\n����\n+\n����\n+\nM\nB\nR\n����\n+\n����\n+\nA\nN\nE\no\nOpen Circuit voltage (Open circuit Voltage):\nVoltage\nbetween terminals in\nZERO CURRENT\nCONDITIONS.\no\n(charge) Maximum Capacity:\nMaximum amount\nof electrical charge stored (Ah).\no\nMass Energy Density: Is the amount of energy that\ncan be stored in a battery per mass unit (kJ/Kg,\nWh/Kg)\nIs\na characteristic of the battery/chemistry.\nThe higher, the better.\no\nPower Density: Power per unit of mass\nthat can be\ndelivered.\no\nCycle Life:\ndefined as the number of\nSTANDARD\ncycles\nthat the battery can undergo before its capacity falls\nbelow 80%.\no\nCurrent rate (C-rate):\nmeasure of the rate at which\na battery is charged or discharged relative to its capacity\n(a\n500mAh battery, being discharged at 5000mA, has\na C-rate of 10C).", "img": "p-002.webp"}, {"n": 3, "title": "Batteries Some figures Typical battery types:", "body": "o\nCharge:\ncurrent amount of electrical charge that can be used to generate power (Ah, mAh)\no\nState of Charge (SOC):\nratio between available capacity\nand maximum capacity\n:\n������\n=\n������������\n������\n∈ [0%, 100%]\n.\no\nNominal Voltage:\nVoltage at 50% SOC\nduring a discharge\ncycle at 0.2C.\n(cell) open circuit voltage\nLithium\n2.5 – 4.3V\nLead\n1.75\nMass energy density\n>500kJ/kg\n140kJ\nPower density\n<2.000W/kg\n180-200W/kg\nCycle life\n2.500-12.000\n50-100\nGasoline:\n44.000kJ/kg\nDiesel fuel:\n40.000kJ/kg\nA typical engine:\n~\n������\n−\n����������\n/\n����", "img": "p-003.webp"}, {"n": 4, "title": "Cells (or batteries) can be connected in series or parallel in order to form battery packs of the desired capacity and v", "body": "Cylindrical\nPrismatic\nPouch\nof\nthe desired capacity and voltage.\nBatteries\nAutomotive\nLead-acid batteries\n- Used as auxiliary battery in vehicles (12-24V).\n- Can be kept at very high state of charge (100%)\nwithout any issue.\n- Low cost, high reliability.\n- Can provide bursts of extremely high current without\nrisk of fire/explosion (thousands of Amps).\n- Resists to high temperatures/shocks.\n- >99% recyclable.\nLithium batteries\n- Used as traction batteries.", "img": "p-004.webp"}, {"n": 5, "title": "Extremely high energy and power density. • Very expensive. • Chance of explosion if overheated, subjected to shock, shor", "body": "- Extremely high energy and power density.\n- Chance of explosion if overheated, subjected to shock, short circuited.\nNi-mh batteries\n- .Used as traction batteries in some hybrid powertrains\n(Toyota Prius until 2015)\n- Inexpensive, safe, reliable.\n- Lower energy density compared to Li-based batteries.\n- Subject to mild (reversible) lazy battery effect.", "img": "p-005.webp"}, {"n": 6, "title": "Batteries Discharge curves", "body": "1) The voltage at the battery terminals\nlowers as\nthe discharge current (C-rate) increases\n. 2) The area\nbelow the curve (harvested energy)\nreduces with increasing current. 3) Not all batteries behave in the same way, battery design can be optimized for maximum capacity (top\nchart) or maximum available current (bottom chart).\nStatements 1 and 3 suggest the\nsimplest battery model:\nan ideal generator with a resistor in series (ESR)\n.\nBoth\n��\n����\nand\n��\ndepend on\nSOC and temperature.\nBattery actual capacity is strongly affected by operating\ntemperature as well", "img": "p-006.webp"}, {"n": 7, "title": "Batteries Aging", "body": "o\nAnother factor that strongly affects the battery\nbehaviour is the\nnumber of cycles that the battery has underwent\n(ageing)\n.\nAn\naged battery shows a higher internal resistance (top left)\no\nThe internal resistance affects the battery self-heating\nduring\noperation, aged batteries produce more heat in both charging and\ndischarging.\no\nAged batteries are subject to higher voltage drop\nwhen\ndischarged\n(at constant C-rate) and higher voltage\nraise when\ncharged (at constant C-rate)\no\nBattery capacity reduces dramatically as the number\nof cycles\nincreases (left bottom)", "img": "p-007.webp"}, {"n": 8, "title": "Batteries Wrap-up", "body": "o\nThe charge-discharge bandwidth affects the battery aging rate (left\nbottom).\no\nTemperature affects the battery aging rate as well\n(right bottom): high temperatures increase battery\ndegradation.\no\nThe battery degradation strongly increases if the\nbattery is\ncharged\nat high currents (high voltages,\ntop-right).\no\nBatteries shall operate at room temperature (20-30°C)\nin order to suffer the smallest\ndegradation\npossible and deliver optimal performance", "img": "p-008.webp"}, {"n": 9, "title": "Batteries Series and parallel", "body": "- Too cold:\nreduced capacity, reduced drainable/injectable current (high ESR).\n- Too hot:\nhigh degradation but high performance.\nTemperatures above 55°C are dangerous.\no\nThe battery system shall be designed in order to\nlimit current:\n- Reduce ohmic losses\n- Reduce heating (and thus, degradation)\n- Exploit the maximum battery capacity\n- Reduce battery degradation during charge\no\nESR estimation can give indication about battery\ndegradation\no\nSince battery temperature has a significant impact\non battery operation (and due to safety\nconcerns),\nthe temperature and temperature gradients\ninside battery packs have to be controlled.\nSame for\ntemperature of different battery packs (in\ncase of several battery packs).\nParallel\n��\n=\n��\n=\n��\n=\n��", "img": "p-009.webp"}, {"n": 10, "title": "�� + �� ��", "body": "��1\n+\n��1\nIn order to keep the ohmic losses low, higher voltage levels are\n��1\ndesirable for power systems.", "img": "p-010.webp"}, {"n": 11, "title": "�� �� �� �� �� ��", "body": "Battery packs are normally formed by several batteries connected in\nseries in order to increase the output voltage (up to 800V in some\napplications).\n��\nThe capacity and maximum drainable current of a string of batteries\n��\n��2\nin\nseries\nis\nideally\nthe same of the single battery\n.\n+\n��2\n��\nElements in series share the same current.\n��2\n��\n+\n��\nCapacity and drainable current can be limited by connecting\n��\nbatteries (or strings of series-connected batteries)\nin parallel.\n+\n=\n��\nElements in parallel share the same voltage.\n��\n��3\n+\n��3\n��\nElements in parallel can virtually be decoupled if the main\n��\n��3", "img": "p-011.webp"}, {"n": 12, "title": "+ Batteries Series In a battery pack with many cells in series, one faulty cell can jeopardize the usability of the whol", "body": "constraint of the power system is the supply voltage.\n=\n��\n- +\n��\n=\n��\n=\n��\n=\n��\nDischarge\n��\nCharge\n��\nIn case of a more\ndegraded battery (the\nmiddle one in figure), or\nwith a lower SOC, or\njust with a lower\ntemperature in a\nstring\nin\n, the common\ncurrent flowing through\nall the batteries\nwill\ncause a higher battery\nheating.\nAt high currents, if the\nESR is very high,\nundervoltage on the\nweak battery may\noccur.\nAnalogously, during\ncharge, overvoltage\non the weak battery\nmay occur.\nIf the internal resistance\nof the battery is\nsignificantly higher, this\ncan limit the maximum\ndrainable/injectable\ncurrent.\nwhole battery\npack!", "img": "p-012.webp"}, {"n": 13, "title": "�� = �� �� �� = �� �� �� = �� �� - + �� �� �� �� = �� �� �� = �� �� �� = ��", "body": "��\n=\n��\n=\n��\n=\n��\n- +\n��1\n��2\n��3\n��\n=\n��\n=\n��\n=\n��", "img": "p-013.webp"}, {"n": 14, "title": "�� + - �� �� �� Batteries Parallel", "body": "��\n+\n- ��1\n��2\n��3\nIn the case of some Battery Electric\nVehicles, there amay be two or more\nbattery packs connected in parallel\non a HV bus through\nhigh\nvoltage\ncontactors (high voltage relays)\n.\nIn case of a battery with a lower\nSOC, or just with a lower\ntemperature (both causing a lower\nVoc) in a\nparallel\n, the batteries\nwith\na higher voltage force a current\ntowards the lower voltage\nbattery\nwith no load on the HV bus as the\ncontactors are closed\n(backcurrent)\n.\nDuring charge, the increased\nvoltage difference between the HV\nbus and the battery pack can force\nseveral amps on the weak battery\npack (overcurrent).\nDuring discharge, the current\nprovided by the weak battery pack\nis lower than the healthy battery\npacks, that can undergo\noverload.", "img": "p-014.webp"}, {"n": 15, "title": "�� �� �� + - Batteries Temperature control", "body": "Discharge\n��\n��1\n��\n��2\n��\n��3\n+\n- Real case:\n- 3 BPs made by 108 cells in series\n(18650, max cont. Disch. curr. 20 A ,\nmax cont. charge current 10A).\n- ~356 − 453\n��\noutput\nvoltage\n(0-100% SOC)\n- 3.7Ω ÷ 4.5Ω\noverall\nESR.\no\nThe temperature of the batteries shall be kept between\ncertain\nbounds to assure efficient, safe and long\noperation.", "img": "p-015.webp"}, {"n": 16, "title": "o The temperature gradients inside a battery pack and the temperature differences between different battery packs shall ", "body": "o\nThe\nbe minimum.\no\nBatteries in BEV (battery electric vehicles) and\nsome PHEV\n(plugin hybrid electric vehicles) require\na\nwater cooling system\nsimilar to that of conventional\nvehicles.\no\nThe desired temperature level is lower, so\nadditional\ncooling\nthrough an active refrigeration system may\nbe necessary (like\nA/C system).\no\nExtremely low temperatures may cause the unusability\nof lithium batteries or the impossibility to recharge\nthem.\nBattery heating system is required.\no\nActive\ncooling and heating require energy.\no\nIn some applications, the battery cooling shares\nthe cooling system with the cabin A/C system", "img": "p-016.webp"}, {"n": 17, "title": "Battery Management System (BMS)", "body": "The Battery Management System is the ECU delegated to managing:\n- Battery (or batteries) connection/disconnection\nto the HV bus.\n- Battery balancing\n- Thermal management\n- Cooling request (the ECU managing the cooling system\nis separate and could be on a separate network)\n- Heating request (in BEVs the battery heaters are\nnormally directly controlled by the BMS).\n- monitoring\n- SOC estimation (open circuit voltage only gives\na vague indication on the actual SOC).\n- SOH (state of health) estimation (many parameters\ncan be monitored along with ESR, described my more", "img": "p-017.webp"}, {"n": 18, "title": "BEV", "body": "accurate models).\n- Maximum\nCHARGE\n/discharge currents.\n- Battery diagnosis\n- Over/undervoltage\n- Overcurrent\n- Overtemperature\n- Insulation measurement\nBEV\nNo emissions (locally)\nLarge battery required for acceptable range (application dependent)\nNo waste heat available (heating have to be actively provided).", "img": "p-018.webp"}, {"n": 19, "title": "Strictly confidential. All contents in this document are property of Kineton s.r.l. 2018", "body": "", "img": "p-019.webp"}, {"n": 20, "title": "REEV/SERIES HYBRID", "body": "Fixed point thermal engine operation (and battery charger) – MAXIMUM\nEFFICIENCY\nMaximum energy recuperation (thanks to the supercapacitor)–\nMAXIMUM EFFICIENCY\nReduced battery size (depends if the thermal engine is small – REEV or\nlarge – SHEV ).\nDuplicated power electronics (electric drives, generator and motor)\nAlmost all ICE components are present (tank, auxiliaries, cooling circuit)\nalong with the along with the e-PWT\ncomponents", "img": "p-020.webp"}, {"n": 21, "title": "", "body": "", "img": "p-021.webp"}, {"n": 22, "title": "Hybrid powertrains", "body": "o\nCoupling a conventional powertrain with a battery-driven\nelectric machine.\no\nThe system complexity (and cost) increase heavily\ndepends on the hybrid architecture.\no\nEvery hybrid system architecture has specific requirements\nin terms of electrical machine,\npower electronics and battery size/type.\no\nThe advantages and the additional features gained\nthrough the adoption of hybrid architecture\nhave\nthe downside of creating issues in terms of integration\n(hardware and software).", "img": "p-022.webp"}, {"n": 23, "title": "P0", "body": "P0\n- Easy system integration (but FEAD design shall\nbe\naddressed)\n- EM may (in some architectures) require liquid\ncooling\n- Power electronics often integrated in the EM\n- Reduced battery size\n- Electric propulsion not available\n- Some engine load shift (limited by EM\npower/torque and reduced battery size)", "img": "p-023.webp"}, {"n": 24, "title": "P1", "body": "- Torque sustain during transients\n- Limited energy recuperation\n- 48V bus (no HV system requirements)\n- Increased fuel efficiency (around 10%)\nP1\n- Higher torque than P0 (no belt)\n- High impact on powertrain design\n- Electric machines requires liquid cooling\n- More complex power electronics\n- Reduced battery size\n- Electric propulsion not available\n- Improved engine load shift respect to P0\n- Improved torque fill during transients", "img": "p-024.webp"}, {"n": 25, "title": "P2", "body": "- Limited energy recuperation\n- 48V bus (no HV system requirements)\n- Increased fuel efficiency respect to P0.\n- No more investment on this architecture\nP2\n- Increased energy recuperation potential (ICE can\nbe decoupled from the wheels through the clutch)\n- Electric creep/drive and e-coasting are possible\n- High integration cost\n- Larger battery is required to provide elctric\npropulsion\n- Starter cannot be eliminated to perform start&stop", "img": "p-025.webp"}, {"n": 26, "title": "P3/P4", "body": "(but can be combined with BSG to obtain P0+P2)\n- System efficiency is limited by the transmission\nbetween the EM and the wheels (both propulsion\nand recuperation)\n- Highest energy recuperation petential\n- Electric driving mode possible\n- In P4 configuration, the electric machine is not", "img": "p-026.webp"}, {"n": 27, "title": "P2.5", "body": "on the transmission drive axle, e-AWD is\npossible.\n- Battery, motor, power electronics are larger and\nmore expensive\n- Fuel consumption improvement up to 25% on\nstandard driving cycle (higher during urban\ndriving).\n- Starter is required to start the ICE (if not coupled\nwith P0)\n- A new design is required (no retrofit).\n- Advantages in terms of system efficiency similar to P3\narchitecture\n- The EM is integrated into the transmission\n(on the even gears shaft) • Easy system integration\n(same ICE, main differences are in the transmission) •\nElectric driving mode possible\n- Starter is required to start the ICE (if not coupled with\nP0)", "img": "p-027.webp"}, {"n": 28, "title": "Strictly confidential. All contents in this document are property of Kineton s.r.l. 2018", "body": "", "img": "p-028.webp"}]}